{"request": {"input_type": "document", "model": "voyage-4", "text": "The service delivery lead explains what goes into customer confirmation emails for the company's On-site Audit and Technician Training services: the 10-business-day report deadline promised for audits, and the up-to-twelve-technician limit with certification included for training. The chunk also covers how internal handoff emails are labeled and why safety conclusions are never written before the report is final.\n\nInterviewer: Your team delivers audits and training. What do you put in writing to customers?\n\nService delivery lead: Deadlines, carefully. For an On-site Audit, we promise the report is delivered within 10 business days after the visit, and we put that in the confirmation email. We never promise a shorter time, even when the auditor thinks he can do it faster.\n\nInterviewer: How about training?\n\nService delivery lead: Technician Training includes certification, up to twelve technicians per session. The confirmation email lists who is enrolled and says certification is included. If they want to send fourteen people, that is a second session, and we say so clearly instead of squeezing them in.\n\nInterviewer: How do you write internally?\n\nService delivery lead: Internal handoffs between the sales team and my team always include the ticket number and the site name in the subject line. We run hundreds of visits a year. Without the site name, an internal email is useless.\n\nInterviewer: Are there things your team should never write to a customer?\n\nService delivery lead: Safety conclusions before the report is final. An auditor might see something worrying on site, but nothing about safety goes in an email until the report is reviewed. A preliminary remark in writing can end up in a legal file."}, "vector": [0.037943437695503235, -0.0240782517939806, -0.023560401052236557, -0.021960224956274033, 0.0248832106590271, -0.07386859506368637, -0.008923656307160854, -0.015338307246565819,